## Introduction
Fusarium species are common soil saprophytes and plant pathogens that have also frequently been reported as aetiological agents of opportunistic infections in humans [1]. In immunocompromised patients, the mortality rate exceeds $70\%$ [2]. Globally, Fusarium solani is the species most frequently isolated in clinical setting $(\sim50\%)$ , followed by Fusarium oxysporum $(\sim20\%)$ and Fusarium verticillioides $(\sim20\%)$ [2], the latter species being the most frequently isolated from deep-seated fusariosis [3, 4]. Fusarium spp. are generally resistant in vivo to most antifungal agents [2]. The recommended therapy for fusariosis is amphotericin B (AMB) or its lipid formulations [2]. Other treatment options include voriconazole (VRC) and posaconazole (PSC), which are indicated for patients with refractory disease or those who are intolerant to AMB [5, 6]. However, the use of a single drug for the treatment of systemic fusariosis has not been completely satisfactory [2]. The combinations of AMB with VRC or PSC have shown some beneficial effects in experimental murine infections by $F_{\mathrm{\bullet}}$ solani or $F$ . oxysporum [7, 8]. In addition, clinical data have confirmed the usefulness of AMB combined with VRC in improving the clinical outcome of invasive fusarial infections in those cases where neutropenia was previously reversed [9, 10].
In this study, we have evaluated the efficacy of AMB combined with VRC or PSC in a model of disseminated infection by $F$ . verticillioides in neutropenic mice.
## Materials and Methods
Fungal Isolates
In Vitro Studies
The in vitro susceptibility of both strains was determined by a broth microdilution reference method, according to the CLSI guidelines for filamentous fungi [11]. For PSC, VRC, and AMB, the endpoint criterion used was $100\%$ of inhibition of growth (MIC-0). Paecilomyces variotti ATCC 36257 was used as quality control strain. Drug interactions were assessed using a checkerboard microdilution method [12].
## Animals
Five-week-old male OF1 mice (Charles River, Criffa S.A., Barcelona, Spain), with mean weight of $30~\mathrm{g}$ were used. The animals were housed in standard boxes with corncob bedding and had free access to food and water. All animal experiments were performed in accordance with the highest standards for care and treatment of research animals, as approved by the Animal Welfare Committee of the Universitat Rovira i Virgili.
Drugs
The drugs tested were AMB (Fungizona, BristolMyers), VRC (Vfend, Pfizer), and PSC (Noxafil, Schering-Plough).
Immunosuppression
Mice were immunosuppressed by a single intraperitoneal (i.p.) injection of $200~\mathrm{{mg}}$ of cyclophosphamide per $\mathrm{kg}$ of body weight plus a single intravenous injection of $150~\mathrm{mg}$ of 5-fluorouracil per $\mathrm{kg}$ on the same day of challenge [13].
Infection and Therapy
Mice were challenged with $1\times10^{7}$ CFU in $0.2~\mathrm{ml}$ of sterile saline injected into the lateral tail vein. Preliminary experiments demonstrated that this inoculum was appropriate for producing an acute infection, with $90\%$ of the animals dying within 11 days (data not shown).
The efficacy of the different treatments was evaluated through prolongation of survival and reduction in fungal tissue burden in spleen and kidneys of infected mice. Groups of 20 mice were established for each strain and each treatment. Ten mice were randomly chosen for survival, and the other 10 were used for tissue burden studies.
The different groups were treated as follows: AMB at $3~\mathrm{mg/kg}$ of body weight/dose given intraperitoneally once daily; VRC at $60~\mathrm{{mg/kg}}$ of body weight/ dose given orally once daily; PSC at $100~\mathrm{{mg/kg}}$ of body weight/dose given orally once daily; or AMB plus VRC or PSC at the same doses as monotherapies. From 3 days prior to infection, the mice that received VRC and a control group were given $50\%$ grapefruit juice in place of water [14]. Control animals received no treatment. All treatments began 1 day after challenge, and the therapy lasted for 10 days. Mice survival was evaluated daily for 30 days after challenge.
For tissue burden studies, mice were killed on day 6 after they were infected. The spleen and kidneys were aseptically removed, and the entire organs were homogenized in $1~\mathrm{ml}$ of sterile saline. Serial tenfold dilutions of the homogenates were plated on PDA and incubated for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ Data were expressed as $\log_{10}$ CFU per gram of tissue.
## Statistics
## Results
The in vitro antifungal activities of the drugs tested and their interactions are shown in Table 1. PSC showed one or two dilutions lower MIC values against the two strains of $F_{\mathrm{\bullet}}$ . verticillioides than VRC and AMB. The combinations of AMB plus VRC or PSC showed indifferent interactions against the two strains tested with the exception of AMB plus PSC, which showed a synergistic interaction against the strain FMR 9434.
VRC, for the two strains tested, and AMB for the strain FMR 9434 significantly prolonged survival compared with that for the control group $(P<0.05)$ (Fig. 1). In addition, the most effective regimen for prolonging survival in comparison with the control group was the combination of AMB plus VRC for both strains tested $(P<0.05),$ . However, neither antifungal combination was statistically better than each drug alone in prolonging survival.
The effects of the different treatments on tissue burden studies are shown in Fig. 2. The spleen and kidneys of the control animals infected with each of the strains showed high fungal loads, the spleen being the most affected organ.
None of the monotherapies tested were effective in reducing the fungal load compared to that of the control group $(P<0.05)$ with exception of AMB in spleen and PSC in kidneys of mice infected with strain FMR 9434.
The combination of AMB plus PSC was able to significantly reduce the fungal load in spleen of mice infected with the strain FMR 9434 and in kidneys of those challenged with the strain FMR 8585, compared to that of the control group $(P<0.05)$ . For the strain FMR 8585, the combinations of AMB plus VRC or PSC were statistically better than the respective monotherapies in reducing the fungal load in kidneys. For the strain 9434, the combination of AMB plus PSC significantly reduced the fungal load in spleen in comparison with PSC alone.
## Discussion
We have demonstrated a modest efficacy of VRC and AMB given alone in the treatment of the murine $F_{\mathrm{\bullet}}$ . verticillioides infection. PSC given alone was unable to prolong the survival with respect to the control group, but it was able to reduce the fungal load, though only in kidney of mice infected with one strain in this experimental model. Clinical data showed mortality rates of $55\mathrm{-}70\%$ in neutropenic patients of fusariosis who received antifungal treatment [2, 5, 15]. Although in our study, AMB, VRC, and PSC demonstrated limited activity against this infection, the administration of the combinations AMB plus VRC and AMB plus PSC has shown some degree of effectiveness either by prolonging survival or by decreasing the fungal load. The slight effectiveness of AMB plus VRC agrees with data obtained in a previous experimental study with $F_{\mathrm{\bullet}}$ . solani, in which this combined treatment was able to prolong the survival but was only minimally effective in decreasing the fungal load [8]. Up to now, there has been only one clinical report on the efficacy of AMB plus VRC in a clinical disseminated infection by $F.$ . verticillioides, which was resolved using LAMB plus VRC after failure of LAMB therapy [16]. In a previous experimental study, the combination of AMB plus PSC proved to be effective in the treatment of disseminated infection by $F.$ . oxysporum [7]. There are no clinical data on the use of AMB plus PSC in $F$ . verticillioides infections.
This work confirms that an effective therapy for the treatment of fusariosis does not yet exist. In these infections, regardless of the treatment used, resolving myelosuppression is crucial for a favourable outcome.
